// OpenInference/OpenLLMetry instrumentors flatten message content into indexed
// keys (e.g. `llm.input_messages.0.message.content`), matched by prefix.
var ContentAttributePrefixes = []string{
	"llm.input_messages.",
	"llm.output_messages.",
	"gen_ai.prompt.",
	"gen_ai.completion.",
	"llm.prompts.",
	"llm.prompt_template.",
	"llm.tools.",
	"ai.prompt.",
	"langfuse.observation.metadata.",
	"langfuse.trace.metadata.",
	"traceloop.prompt.template_variables.",
	"lmnr.association.properties.metadata.",
}

// Members of a content prefix that carry identity, not content. Laminar's
// metadata is the caller's arbitrary payload, but the sink reads these three
// as the tool call id and the agent name; masking them would cost a trace
// with content capture off its tool linkage and its agent.
var ContentPrefixIdentityAttributes = map[string]struct{}{
	"lmnr.association.properties.metadata.tool_call_id": {},
	"lmnr.association.properties.metadata.agent.name":   {},
	"lmnr.association.properties.metadata.service.name": {},
}

// Indexed families where only the content leaf is sensitive (siblings like
// `.document.id` / `.document.score` are metadata), matched by suffix.
var ContentAttributeSuffixes = []string{
	".document.content",
	".embedding.text",
}

// The same, for a leaf too generic to match on its own across every
// namespace (gen_ai.agent.description is identity): a prefix and its content
// leaves. Traceloop writes each tool definition as
// llm.request.functions.{i}.{name,description,parameters} (Ollama
// instrumentation 0.62.3; OpenAI, Anthropic and LangChain up to 0.40.x).
// .name stays: identity. .arguments has no emitter in the versions read, but
// is content by name wherever it appears.
var ContentAttributePrefixedSuffixes = []struct {
	Prefix   string
	Suffixes []string
}{
	{"llm.request.functions.", []string{".description", ".parameters", ".arguments"}},
}

// SpanKind -> OTel GenAI gen_ai.operation.name, where a canonical operation exists.
// CHAIN is deliberately absent: the conventions define no operation for a
// generic workflow step, and inventing one would put a non-spec value in a
// spec-defined enum. Revisit only if the upstream invoke_node proposal lands.
// RERANKER maps to rerank, which the conventions do not define either. It is
// the value the Vercel AI SDK's @ai-sdk/otel writes on a rerank call, and the
// SDKs write it on AI SDK v6 reranks too, so it is already on the wire. Mapping
// the kind to it gives every rerank span (AI SDK, OpenInference RERANKER,
// user-created) the same operation, so a filter on either key finds them all.
var OperationByKind = map[string]string{
	SpanKindLLM:       "chat",
	SpanKindTool:      "execute_tool",
	SpanKindEmbedding: "embeddings",
	SpanKindAgent:     "invoke_agent",
	SpanKindRetriever: "retrieval",
	SpanKindReranker:  "rerank",
}

// The inverse, for spans that speak the conventions (and the GenAI-native
// vendor operations listed below, such as agent_step, compaction and rerank).
// A GenAI-native instrumentation sets gen_ai.operation.name and never heard of
// openinference.span.kind, and both keys must be present on every span, so
// the derivation has to run in both directions.
//
// Not a mechanical inversion of the map above, and it cannot be generated from
// it, for two reasons:
//   - several operations share one kind. text_completion and generate_content
//     are LLM calls the same way chat is, and create_agent is an AGENT span
//     just as invoke_agent is, so the mapping is many-to-one and only the
//     chat/invoke_agent entries round-trip.
//   - CHAIN has no operation of its own, but four operations land on it.
//     invoke_workflow and plan are real GenAI operations with no taxonomy
//     value of their own; CHAIN is the honest home for both, and the reverse
//     direction is the only one that can say so. agent_step is not a
//     conventions operation: the Vercel AI SDK's @ai-sdk/otel writes it on
//     each step of an agent run (one model call and the tools it asked
//     for), a step within the run rather than an agent invocation, so
//     CHAIN as well.
//   - compaction is not a conventions operation either. A producer writes it
//     on the span of a step that shrinks the agent's context (a summary
//     replaces older turns, tool results are cleared, old turns are cut). The
//     step is not a model call and not an agent invocation, so neither LLM
//     nor AGENT fits, even when a model writes the summary: the cost of that
//     call, if any, is its own LLM span. CHAIN is the honest home, as for
//     agent_step. The span's attributes are the rius.compaction.* keys.
//   - LLM also takes four operations @ai-sdk/otel writes that no convention
//     defines, kept as written rather than rewritten to chat or given an
//     invented name: decide (the experimental decision call: an operation
//     span and its model call, both a billed call to a decision model, so
//     LLM rather than CHAIN, which would hide the call), and the AI SDK's
//     own operation ids for speech and transcription (ai.generateSpeech,
//     ai.transcribe, ai.streamTranscribe), each one call to a remote audio
//     model. OpenInference has no audio kind, and LLM is its model call.
var KindByOperation = map[string]string{
	"chat":                SpanKindLLM,
	"text_completion":     SpanKindLLM,
	"generate_content":    SpanKindLLM,
	"execute_tool":        SpanKindTool,
	"embeddings":          SpanKindEmbedding,
	"invoke_agent":        SpanKindAgent,
	"create_agent":        SpanKindAgent,
	"retrieval":           SpanKindRetriever,
	"rerank":              SpanKindReranker,
	"invoke_workflow":     SpanKindChain,
	"plan":                SpanKindChain,
	"agent_step":          SpanKindChain,
	"compaction":          SpanKindChain,
	"decide":              SpanKindLLM,
	"ai.generateSpeech":   SpanKindLLM,
	"ai.transcribe":       SpanKindLLM,
	"ai.streamTranscribe": SpanKindLLM,
}

// Which attribute names the target of a span's `{operation} {target}` name,
// per kind. One key each, so a tool name can never surface in an agent's name
// and the composed name stays a reliable filter value. CHAIN is absent: it has
// no operation and so no composed name. LLM reads the REQUEST model on
// purpose: a pending snapshot must carry the same name as the final span.
// RERANKER reads the request model too, as EMBEDDING does, so a rerank span
// is named `rerank <model>`.
var NameTargetByKind = map[string]string{
	SpanKindLLM:       GenAIRequestModel,
	SpanKindEmbedding: GenAIRequestModel,
	SpanKindTool:      GenAIToolName,
	SpanKindAgent:     GenAIAgentName,
	SpanKindRetriever: GenAIDataSourceID,
	SpanKindReranker:  GenAIRequestModel,
}
